// A truncated source must be REJECTED with a parse error — not kill the compiler.
//
// This file ends mid-expression, which is the normal state of a file being typed.
// Before BUGS #164 it did not produce a diagnostic at all: `parse_error` reports
// without consuming a token (deliberately — each caller decides how to
// resynchronize), so a parser out of input kept calling `advance()`, and
// `current()` read `this.tokens[this.pos]` with no bound. Past the end that is
// `IndexError: index 4 out of bounds (length 4)`, and in Saffron an IndexError is
// a *fatal runtime error*, not a diagnostic: it routes to
// `__runtime_error_fatal` and exits before anything is written. Under `--json`
// that meant empty stdout, which the LSP correctly read as an unparseable payload
// and published nothing — an editor showing a clean file for source that cannot
// parse.
//
// The suite could not catch this before because every other input here is a
// complete file. `test/fail/*.sf` are deliberately malformed, but malformed and
// *finished*; none is truncated. This one is, and that is the whole point of it.
//
// KNOW WHAT THIS FILE DOES AND DOES NOT PIN. The fail suite records any nonzero
// exit as a pass (`tools/run_tests.sh:394`), and the pre-fix crash also exited 1
// — so on its own this file does NOT distinguish "rejected with a diagnostic"
// from "died with a fatal IndexError". It pins the weaker property, that the
// compiler must not accept truncated source.
//
// The discriminating test is editors/shared/test/compiler_discovery.test.mjs
// ("incomplete buffers produce diagnostics, not a crash or a hang"): it drives
// six truncated shapes through the real LSP round trip and asserts a *diagnostic
// arrives*, which an empty-stdout crash cannot satisfy. It also bounds the wait,
// so the over-correction — clamping the cursor to EOF turning the crash into a
// hang — fails it rather than passing quietly.
//
// This file's job is to keep the case in the compiler's own suite, where someone
// editing parser.sf will actually run it.
